### Participant C2: Turnstile Justice (Criminal Justice Reform)
**[13:18 - 16:55]**

**Participant:** First I asked a bunch of questions. As noted from my fellow member of Room C, the AI was rather concerned for me because I expressed perhaps some resentment in the writing of my theme, and then offered comfort, I guess.

I asked what it thinks are good approaches, and it recommended free verse, which I was already going to go with. I also asked some weird things such as whether I should add found elements [citations, legal language]. It said that was a good idea and would be interesting.

I fed it a few lines. For example:
*"I weep as I see, turnstile justice, turnstile court,  
Mercy for him, but what of me?  
What of us all, sheep among these wolves?"*

That's where it started to try and comfort me. Then I tried composing a bit and it gave me advice for structure and even gave me a line to add. I then wrote a more full version, and I asked it to refine. So it gave me structured stanzas and also gave me a new final line, which I added to.

After I sent that, it gave me basically a few comments that I found helpful.

**Final Poem:**
*"Turnstile justice, turnstile court,  
Mercy for him, but what of me?  
What of us all, sheep among wolves,  
A gavel rung, a soothing wave—  
But for whom? A scoreboard for wolves.*

*An offset sentence, bailed out for naught,  
Promises of reform, but only drought comes.  
They said they would reform—with rehabilitation—  
But the system let them back in, rested, bold,  
To this pen of us sheep, who die for this test.*

*The wolves lunge forth, fresh from the bench,  
A culling of those who were never asked.  
I weep as I see, turnstile justice—  
Whom does it serve?"*

